<main class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-3 sm:px-6 py-4 sm:py-6">
<div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow p-4 sm:p-8">
<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-6">About Tesserae</h2>
<div class="prose max-w-none space-y-8">
<section>
<h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">What is Tesserae?</h3>
<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">Tesserae is a free, open tool for discovering connections in ancient literature. It began as a search for intertexts, the places where one author echoes, adapts or quotes another, and it still does that. Give it two works and it finds the passages that share words, phrases, rare vocabulary, sound, syntax or meaning, in one language or across languages. Around that search it has grown into a place to read. The Reader shows a text with its parallels, similar passages, later quotations, commentary and translation at the side. Theme Search finds the passages about a scene or an idea you describe in English, in every language of the corpus at once. Search One Text asks a single work what it says about a subject and shows where it says it. Beside the literature stand inscriptions and papyri, coins, museum objects and dated events, so a passage can be read next to the documents and things of its world.</p>
<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">The corpus holds more than 3,500 texts in Latin, Greek, English, Hebrew, Coptic, Persian and Urdu, from Homer to the Renaissance and beyond, with an English translation wherever a public-domain one exists. Founded in 2008 by Neil Coffee (Department of Classics) and <a href="https://arts-sciences.buffalo.edu/linguistics/faculty/jean-pierre-koenig.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-amber-600 hover:underline">J.&nbsp;P. Koenig</a> (Department of Linguistics) at the University at Buffalo, SUNY, Tesserae is now in its sixth version, rebuilt from the ground up around the scoring its earlier versions were known for.</p>
<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The name "Tesserae" refers to the small tiles used in Roman mosaics. Just as these tiles combine to form larger images, textual parallels reveal the interconnected nature of classical literature.</p>
</section>
<section class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-lg p-5">
<h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-amber-900 mb-3">Creation &amp; Credits</h3>
<p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">
<strong>Tesserae V6</strong> was created in January 2026 by <a href="https://arts-sciences.buffalo.edu/classics/faculty/core-faculty/coffee-neil.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-amber-600 hover:underline font-semibold">Neil Coffee</a> (University at Buffalo).</p>
<p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Tesserae is a collaboration between <a href="https://arts-sciences.buffalo.edu/classics/faculty/core-faculty/coffee-neil.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-amber-600 hover:underline font-semibold">Neil Coffee</a> (University at Buffalo) and <a href="https://engineering.nd.edu/faculty/walter-scheirer/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-amber-600 hover:underline font-semibold">Walter Scheirer</a> (University of Notre Dame). Neil created V6 and the team collaborates on its ongoing development.</p>
<p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">
<a href="https://mta.ca/directory/chris-forstall" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-amber-600 hover:underline font-semibold">Chris Forstall</a> (Mount Allison University) was the lead developer of Tesserae V3, which established the core algorithms and infrastructure that V6 builds upon.</p>
<p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Graduate Tesserae Fellows have been at the core of the project from its inception. In chronological order from the beginning of the project in 2008, they are (UB = U. Buffalo graduate student, ND = Notre Dame graduate student): Poornima Shakthi (UB), Roelant Ossewarde (UB), Chris Forstall (UB), James Gawley (UB), Jeffery Kinnison (ND), Tessa Little (UB), Nozomu Okuda (UB), Joseph Miller (UB), Travis Qualls (UB), John James (UB), Abby Swenor (ND).</p>
<div class="text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-2">
<p>
<strong>Built upon:</strong>
</p>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside ml-2 space-y-1">
<li>
<strong>Tesserae V3</strong>: Original scoring algorithm, text corpus, and synonym dictionaries (developed by Chris Forstall at University at Buffalo)</li>
<li>
<strong>Tesserae V5</strong>: API design concepts and modern architecture patterns (developed with Walter Scheirer)</li>
</ul>
<div class="mt-3 space-y-1">
<p>
<strong>Code:</strong> MIT License — free to use, modify, and redistribute.</p>
<p>
<strong>Original datasets</strong> (benchmark sets, gold standards, translation alignments): CC BY 4.0 — free to use with attribution.</p>
<p>
<strong>Corpus texts:</strong> individual licenses per source; see the Text Credits section for provenance and terms.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="how-to-cite">
<h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">How to Cite</h3>
<div class="text-sm text-gray-700 space-y-2">
<p>If Tesserae contributed to published research, please cite the project:</p>
<p class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded p-3 font-mono text-xs">Coffee, N., &amp; The Tesserae Project Team (2026). Tesserae V6: Literary and Historical Discovery (Version 6.0) [Software]. https://tesserae.caset.buffalo.edu</p>
<p>GitHub’s “Cite this repository” button on <a href="https://github.com/tesserae/tesserae-v6" class="text-red-600 hover:underline">the project repository</a> provides BibTeX and APA forms. A journal article describing V6 is under review and will become the preferred citation on acceptance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section>
<h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">Key Features</h3>
<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
<div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
<h4 class="font-medium text-red-700 mb-2">Intertextual Search</h4>
<p class="text-sm text-gray-600">Compare two works, or one work against the whole corpus. Lemma, exact form, sound, edit distance, semantic embeddings, dictionary synonyms, syntax, rare words and quotation are weighed together, and every result shows what it rests on.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
<h4 class="font-medium text-red-700 mb-2">Across Languages</h4>
<p class="text-sm text-gray-600">A passage finds its counterpart in another language through dictionaries and a model of meaning, between Greek and Latin, Latin and English, Hebrew and Greek, and Coptic and Greek.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
<h4 class="font-medium text-red-700 mb-2">Theme Search</h4>
<p class="text-sm text-gray-600">Describe a scene or an idea in English and get the passages about it, from every language at once, ranked by meaning and marked with how confident the match is.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
<h4 class="font-medium text-red-700 mb-2">The Reader</h4>
<p class="text-sm text-gray-600">Read a text with a gutter that marks every line’s connections. Open its parallels, similar passages, quotations in later writers, commentary notes, articles and a translation beside it.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
<h4 class="font-medium text-red-700 mb-2">Search One Text</h4>
<p class="text-sm text-gray-600">Ask one work what it says about a subject. The passages come back in order of fit, on a strip that shows where in the work they fall, with a short account of what the work says and what the commentaries say.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
<h4 class="font-medium text-red-700 mb-2">History</h4>
<p class="text-sm text-gray-600">Inscriptions and papyri, coins, museum objects and dated events, searchable beside the literature they quote or echo, each linked to its source collection.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section>
<h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">The Corpus</h3>
<div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 mb-4">
<div class="text-center p-4 bg-red-50 rounded-lg">
<div class="text-3xl font-bold text-red-700">1,444+</div>
<div class="text-sm text-gray-600">Latin Texts</div>
</div>
<div class="text-center p-4 bg-amber-50 rounded-lg">
<div class="text-3xl font-bold text-amber-700">650+</div>
<div class="text-sm text-gray-600">Greek Texts</div>
</div>
<div class="text-center p-4 bg-amber-50 rounded-lg">
<div class="text-3xl font-bold text-amber-700">14+</div>
<div class="text-sm text-gray-600">English Texts</div>
</div>
<div class="text-center p-4 bg-green-50 rounded-lg">
<div class="text-3xl font-bold text-green-700">186+</div>
<div class="text-sm text-gray-600">Coptic Texts</div>
</div>
</div>
<p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mb-3">Our corpus draws from authoritative sources including Perseus Digital Library, CSEL, and other scholarly editions. Provenance information is tracked for each text.</p>
</section>
<section>
<button class="w-full text-left bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-lg p-5 hover:bg-amber-100 hover:border-amber-300 transition-colors group cursor-pointer">
<div class="flex items-center justify-between">
<h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-amber-800 group-hover:text-amber-900">Text Credits and Provenance</h3>
<svg class="w-5 h-5 text-amber-600 group-hover:translate-x-1 transition-transform" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
<path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7">
</path>
</svg>
</div>
<p class="text-sm text-amber-700 mt-2">View the sources and licences of every collection (texts, translations, inscriptions and papyri, scholarship), and the electronic and print source of every text in our corpus, with links to the original databases.</p>
</button>
</section>
<section>
<button class="w-full text-left bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-lg p-5 hover:bg-amber-100 hover:border-amber-300 transition-colors group cursor-pointer">
<div class="flex items-center justify-between">
<h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-amber-800 group-hover:text-amber-900">Research</h3>
<svg class="w-5 h-5 text-amber-600 group-hover:translate-x-1 transition-transform" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
<path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7">
</path>
</svg>
</div>
<p class="text-sm text-amber-700 mt-2">The studies, evaluations and articles behind the site: how the matching is scored, how well it works, and what was measured when a method changed.</p>
</button>
</section>
<section>
<h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">Technical Details</h3>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-700 space-y-2">
<li>Lemmatization via lookup tables built from Universal Dependencies treebanks, with CLTK (Classical Language Toolkit) as the fallback for Latin and Greek; Stanza for Hebrew, Persian and Urdu</li>
<li>NLTK for English text processing</li>
<li>A passage index of about 700,000 windows, each with a model-written description and an embedding, behind Theme Search, Similar Passages and the Reader</li>
<li>SPhilBERTa model for cross-lingual semantic similarity</li>
<li>Universal Dependencies treebank support for syntax analysis</li>
<li>Metrical scansion data integrated from MQDQ/Pede Certo</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-lg p-5">
<h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-amber-900 mb-4">Grant Support &amp; Acknowledgments</h3>
<p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">The Tesserae Project has been made possible by the generous support of the following organizations:</p>
<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-4">
<div class="bg-white p-4 rounded-lg border border-amber-200">
<a href="https://www.neh.gov/divisions/odh" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="flex items-center gap-3 hover:opacity-80">
<img src="/logos/neh-logo.jpg" alt="NEH Logo" class="w-20 h-auto object-contain flex-shrink-0">
<div>
<div class="font-semibold text-gray-900">National Endowment for the Humanities</div>
<div class="text-sm text-gray-600">Office of Digital Humanities</div>
<div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Digital Humanities Advancement Grant (2018-2020)</div>
<div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Start-Up Phase II Grant (2012-2013)</div>
</div>
</a>
</div>
<div class="bg-white p-4 rounded-lg border border-amber-200">
<a href="https://arts-sciences.buffalo.edu/classics.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="flex items-center gap-3 hover:opacity-80">
<img src="/logos/ub-logo.png" alt="University at Buffalo Logo" class="w-20 h-auto object-contain flex-shrink-0">
<div>
<div class="font-semibold text-gray-900">University at Buffalo, SUNY</div>
<div class="text-sm text-gray-600">Department of Classics</div>
<div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Institutional home and ongoing support</div>
</div>
</a>
</div>
<div class="bg-white p-4 rounded-lg border border-amber-200">
<a href="https://www.snf.ch/en" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="flex items-center gap-3 hover:opacity-80">
<img src="/logos/snsf-logo.png" alt="Swiss National Science Foundation Logo" class="w-24 h-auto object-contain flex-shrink-0">
<div>
<div class="font-semibold text-gray-900">Swiss National Science Foundation</div>
<div class="text-sm text-gray-600">Intertextuality in Flavian Epic Poetry</div>
<div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Research Grant (2013-2016)</div>
</div>
</a>
</div>
<div class="bg-white p-4 rounded-lg border border-amber-200">
<a href="https://www.fmsh.fr/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="flex items-center gap-3 hover:opacity-80">
<img src="/logos/fmsh-logo.png" alt="Fondation Maison des Sciences de l'Homme Logo" class="w-20 h-auto object-contain flex-shrink-0">
<div>
<div class="font-semibold text-gray-900">Fondation Maison des Sciences de l'Homme</div>
<div class="text-sm text-gray-600">Transatlantic Program for Digital Humanities</div>
<div class="text-xs text-gray-500">TESSERAE MUSIVAE Project (2016-2017)</div>
</div>
</a>
</div>
<div class="bg-white p-4 rounded-lg border border-amber-200">
<a href="https://aws.amazon.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="flex items-center gap-3 hover:opacity-80">
<div class="w-16 h-16 bg-orange-500 rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0">
<span class="text-white font-bold text-sm">AWS</span>
</div>
<div>
<div class="font-semibold text-gray-900">Amazon Web Services</div>
<div class="text-sm text-gray-600">Cloud Computing Resources</div>
<div class="text-xs text-gray-500">In-kind grant (2016-2017)</div>
</div>
</a>
</div>
<div class="bg-white p-4 rounded-lg border border-amber-200">
<a href="https://arts-sciences.buffalo.edu/humanities-institute.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="flex items-center gap-3 hover:opacity-80">
<div class="w-16 h-16 bg-blue-800 rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0">
<span class="text-white font-bold text-xs">UBHI</span>
</div>
<div>
<div class="font-semibold text-gray-900">UB Humanities Institute</div>
<div class="text-sm text-gray-600">Digital Humanities Institute at Buffalo</div>
<div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Seed Grants &amp; Fellowships (2008-2020)</div>
</div>
</a>
</div>
</div>
<p class="text-sm text-gray-600 italic">We are grateful to all our supporters who have made this research possible over the years.</p>
</section>
<section>
<h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">Contact</h3>
<p class="text-gray-700">Questions or feedback? Contact <a href="mailto:ncoffee@buffalo.edu" class="text-amber-600 hover:underline">ncoffee@buffalo.edu</a>
</p>
<p class="text-gray-700 mt-2">
<strong>Neil Coffee</strong>, Department of Classics, University at Buffalo</p>
</section>
<section>
<h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">Citation</h3>
<div class="bg-gray-100 p-4 rounded font-mono text-sm">Coffee, Neil, Walter Scheirer, et al. Tesserae V6: Literary and Historical Discovery. University at Buffalo, 2026. https://tesserae.caset.buffalo.edu</div>
<p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mt-3">Please cite the full project on first reference, together with the specific tool, corpus text, or query you used. Include the version (currently ) and your access date, since the corpus and tools are updated regularly.</p>
</section>
<section>
<h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">What the site records</h3>
<p class="text-gray-700">To understand how the site is used, it records which pages a browser opens during one visit, the network address and the city and country that address suggests, and the searches run. A visit is one browser tab's session, identified by a random token that is discarded when the tab closes. No account or personal detail is involved, and nothing is sent to an outside analytics service.</p>
</section>
<section class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-lg p-5">
<h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-amber-900 mb-3">Text Sources</h3>
<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The texts used in this project were gathered from many electronic text databases. Latin and Greek texts come from <a href="http://thelatinlibrary.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-red-700 hover:underline font-medium">The Latin Library</a>, <a href="http://www.perseus.tufts.edu/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-red-700 hover:underline font-medium">The Perseus Project</a>, <a href="https://digiliblt.uniupo.it/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-red-700 hover:underline font-medium">DigilibLT</a>, <a href="https://opengreekandlatin.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-red-700 hover:underline font-medium">Open Greek and Latin Project</a>, <a href="http://www.mqdq.it/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-red-700 hover:underline font-medium">Musisque Deoque</a>, and <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20220305141011/http://www.forumromanum.org/literature/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-red-700 hover:underline font-medium">Corpus Scriptorum Latinorum</a>. The Greek New Testament is <a href="https://sblgnt.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-red-700 hover:underline font-medium">The Greek New Testament: SBL Edition</a> (SBLGNT), edited by Michael W. Holmes, ©2010 <a href="http://sbl-site.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-red-700 hover:underline font-medium">Society of Biblical Literature</a> and <a href="http://logos.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-red-700 hover:underline font-medium">Logos Bible Software</a>, licensed under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0. Coptic texts (Sahidic and Bohairic) come from <a href="https://copticscriptorium.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-red-700 hover:underline font-medium">Coptic Scriptorium</a> (CC-BY 4.0; the Sahidica New Testament is additionally subject to its own academic-use license, ©2000–2006 J. Warren Wells). The Hebrew Bible text is the <a href="https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%9E%D7%A9%D7%AA%D7%9E%D7%A9:Dovi/%D7%9E%D7%A7%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A4%D7%99_%D7%94%D7%9E%D7%A1%D7%95%D7%A8%D7%94" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-red-700 hover:underline font-medium">Miqra according to the Masorah</a> (MAM) edition, based on the Aleppo Codex, obtained through <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-red-700 hover:underline font-medium">Sefaria</a> (CC-BY-SA); Hebrew morphological lemmatization draws on the <a href="https://etcbc.github.io/bhsa/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-red-700 hover:underline font-medium">ETCBC/BHSA</a> dataset (Biblia Hebraica Stuttgartensia Amstelodamensis; CC-BY-NC 4.0, DOI 10.17026/dans-z6y-skyh) via Text-Fabric. The Hebrew semantic channel uses <a href="https://huggingface.co/davidmsmiley/MiqraBERT" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-red-700 hover:underline font-medium">MiqraBERT</a> (D. M. Smiley), fine-tuned in-house on <a href="https://www.openbible.info/labs/cross-references/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-red-700 hover:underline font-medium">OpenBible.info</a> cross-references (CC-BY, from the Treasury of Scripture Knowledge); Hebrew-to-Greek Septuagint matching uses the <a href="https://ccat.sas.upenn.edu/rak/catss.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-red-700 hover:underline font-medium">CATSS</a> Masoretic-Septuagint parallel (E. Tov), bridged through Greek to the Latin Vulgate for Hebrew-to-Latin matching. We have modified the texts by changing the markup, and may have made superficial changes to orthography. During our searches, all punctuation and capitalization are removed.</p>
</section>
</div>
</div>
</main>
<footer class="bg-gray-100 border-t mt-8 py-4">
<div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 text-center text-sm text-gray-500">
<p>Tesserae V6</p>
</div>
</footer>
</div>"
`;

