</li>
</ul>
<p class="mt-4 pt-3 border-t border-gray-200 px-3 text-xs">
<button type="button" class="text-gray-500 hover:text-red-700 hover:underline">Suggest a change</button>
</p>
</nav>
<div class="flex-1 p-6">
<div class="prose max-w-none">
<h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 pb-2 border-b border-gray-200 mb-4">Start here</h3>
<p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Tesserae finds passages that echo one another in Latin, Greek, English, Coptic, Hebrew, Persian and Urdu, whether through shared words, shared sounds, or a shared subject. It offers eight kinds of search, listed below in the order of the "What are you trying to do?" panel. The Search One Text page, where a visit starts, and the Intertextual Search page show that panel the first time you visit. Choosing an answer takes you to the right search, and "Skip" closes the panel for good. The "Start here" link at the top of every page brings it back, and Tessa gives the same list when you ask her where to start.</p>
<div class="space-y-5">
<div class="border-l-4 border-gray-300 pl-4">
<h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Phrase Search</h4>
<p class="text-gray-600 text-sm mt-1">Compares a source text with a target text and ranks the passages they share. The default match type is Fusion, which combines eleven detection methods. Use it to discover allusions, quotations and echoes between two works. See <button class="text-red-600 hover:underline">Intertextual Search, how it works</button> and <button class="text-red-600 hover:underline">Match types</button>.</p>
</div>
<div class="border-l-4 border-gray-300 pl-4">
<h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Line Search</h4>
<p class="text-gray-600 text-sm mt-1">Finds every line in the corpus that shares vocabulary with one line, chosen from a text or typed in. Match on dictionary forms, on identical forms, or with a regular expression, and Greek may be typed with or without accents. For example, "arma virumque cano" finds every line sharing "arma" and "vir". See <button class="text-red-600 hover:underline">Match types</button>.</p>
</div>
<div class="border-l-4 border-gray-300 pl-4">
<h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Rare Words, Rare Pairs and String Search</h4>
<p class="text-gray-600 text-sm mt-1">Rare Words lists words found in fewer than 50 texts that your two texts share, and Rare Pairs lists unusual word pairs that few texts contain together. Both suggest direct borrowing or a stylistic fingerprint. Export CSV saves the list you see, and Download everything (CSV) fetches every place each word occurs in the corpus, which can be a large file. String Search looks for words, patterns and combinations across the whole corpus, with wildcards and the operators AND, OR, NOT and proximity. The patterns are explained under <button class="text-red-600 hover:underline">Match types</button>, and <button class="text-red-600 hover:underline">Search Tips</button> suggests when to try the rare-word modes.</p>
</div>
<div class="border-l-4 border-gray-300 pl-4">
<h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Theme Search</h4>
<p class="text-gray-600 text-sm mt-1">Finds passages on a subject you describe in your own words, in every indexed language at once, even when they share no vocabulary with your description. Use it when you do not know what words a scene is phrased in. See <button class="text-red-600 hover:underline">Theme Search</button>.</p>
</div>
<div class="border-l-4 border-gray-300 pl-4">
<h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Search One Text</h4>
<p class="text-gray-600 text-sm mt-1">Asks one work what it says about a subject, in plain words. It is Theme Search run inside that one work, and the closest passages come back. See <button class="text-red-600 hover:underline">Search One Text</button>.</p>
</div>
<div class="border-l-4 border-gray-300 pl-4">
<h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Reader</h4>
<p class="text-gray-600 text-sm mt-1">Shows a text with a gutter marking where the rest of the corpus connects to each line, and a panel with those connections, the translation and the scholarship on the passage. See <button class="text-red-600 hover:underline">The Reader</button>.</p>
</div>
<div class="border-l-4 border-gray-300 pl-4">
<h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">People &amp; Places</h4>
<p class="text-gray-600 text-sm mt-1">Lists every line where a person, place or god is named, in Latin, Greek and English. See <button class="text-red-600 hover:underline">People &amp; Places</button>.</p>
</div>
<div class="border-l-4 border-gray-300 pl-4">
<h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Collections</h4>
<p class="text-gray-600 text-sm mt-1">Adds inscriptions, papyri, events, coins and museum objects, switched on from the top row. See <button class="text-red-600 hover:underline">Collections, Beta marks and other controls</button>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-2">Choosing a language and two texts</h4>
<p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">The Intertextual Search page has one tab for each language (Latin, Greek, English, Coptic, Hebrew, Persian and Urdu) and a Cross-Language tab for a pair of languages. Seven pairs are open. If a pair is ever held back for rebuilding, the page says so under the pair buttons and the pair returns when the rebuild is done. No pair is held at present. The author, work and book menus accept typing, so a few letters of a name will find it. Choose a <strong>source</strong>, usually the earlier text, and a <strong>target</strong> that may echo it, and click "Find Parallels". Results are ranked by confidence, with the matched words highlighted and badges showing which methods found each pair. See <button class="text-red-600 hover:underline">Cross-Language Search</button> and <button class="text-red-600 hover:underline">Reading the results</button>.</p>
<div class="mt-4 bg-amber-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
<p class="text-amber-700 text-sm">
<strong>Tip.</strong> Start with a smaller section, such as Book 1. Large comparisons, such as the whole Aeneid against the Metamorphoses, take longer on the first run, and later searches of the same pair are cached.</p>
</div>
<div class="mt-4 bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
<p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">
<strong>Example:</strong> Compare Vergil's Aeneid Book 1 (source) with Lucan's Civil War Book 1 (target) to find how Lucan echoes Vergil.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</main>
<footer class="bg-gray-100 border-t mt-8 py-4">
<div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 text-center text-sm text-gray-500">
<p>Tesserae V6</p>
</div>
</footer>
</div>"
`;

